  "description": "Our LSA port: the E33p 'lsa' arm (scripts/run_e33n.py --arm lsa). The testbed with NO page writer and NO notebook: only the reading layer (L2) changes, to LSA's released native read (par_global_read lsa). Each closed block of 512 keys is grouped by similarity into buckets of about 8 (max 16; keys summed in groups of 4 before grouping); a query scores one centre per bucket with its best head, opens the best buckets' keys up to 512, and also reads 4 sink keys (the row's first tokens) and the last 512 tokens. No new weights; no gradient through the choice. L2 and L3 loop 4 rounds with shared weights, as in every arm. Trained like every arm of the testbed: 1.5B tokens at 4k, then 0.2B at 16k (the decay), same data (FineWeb + LongDocs-lite), seed 1 on AWS. Result: recovers 99 % of the window-to-dense gap at 16k and beats dense at 128k. Parameters: 68,701,396 in total, 26,260,308 without the word tables (input table 13,493,056 + output layer 28,948,032); 3,985,856 fewer than the notebook arms, which carry the writer; the trainer's count and the trained checkpoint's stored weights give the same number. The testbed is called '30M' after the notebook arms' 30.2M without word tables (Kaplan et al.'s convention).",
